# Build output
/build/
build*/
cmake-build-*/

# Fetched tooling
/.tools/

# JUCE fetch cache (if any local)
_deps/

# The `linux-clang` job tees the compiler output here, in the workspace root, so
# `check-clang-warnings.py` can classify it. Reproducing that job locally the way
# docs/procedures/CI_CD.md describes leaves the same file behind.
/clang-build.log

# The compiler cache. Every Ninja job in build.yml sets CCACHE_DIR to
# `${{ github.workspace }}/.ccache`, i.e. inside the tree, and so does anyone
# reproducing one of those builds locally. On a hosted runner the directory is
# never at risk -- checkout runs before the cache restore in every job, so there
# is nothing to clean away -- and it stays out of the artifacts because every
# packaging step names `build/...` and `dist/...` explicitly rather than globbing
# the root. This entry is for the same reason as /dist/ and /clang-build.log
# above: a few hundred megabytes of untracked object cache in `git status` is how
# a real change gets missed in the read before a commit.
/.ccache/

# CI staging. Every build.yml packaging step writes here (dist/Anamorph-<OS>,
# dist/Anamorph-<OS>-debug, the two installers), and so does anyone reproducing a
# packaging step locally the way docs/procedures/CI_CD.md describes. Untracked
# build output showing up as untracked files is how a real change gets missed in
# a `git status` read before a commit.
/dist/

# Editor / OS cruft
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
*.swp

# Python bytecode. Running the scripts/check-*.py checkers as scripts does NOT
# create this -- CPython writes no cache for __main__. It appears when one is
# imported instead, which is how a checker gets exercised from an ad-hoc
# verification snippet (importlib against scripts/check-docs.py).
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
